# The frag grenade (frag.tscn, a CbItem: every simulation agrees on its body): a sphere 18 cm across,
# 0.4 kg, bouncy (0.45), with some rolling and air drag (0.2 each) so it settles near where it lands.
# It goes to slot 6 if that is free, out of sight, three to a stack; its own key (H) throws one from
# wherever it is carried, or select it and press use. The mod gives them one at a time, a little
# apart, until there are three (frag.cpp). Its Blast (an Area3D, a ball 8 m round) is the region the
# blast throws things in; how far it hurts is the mod's frag.radius. (This note lives here: Godot drops
# a scene's comments when it saves it.)
#
# The throw: its key plays the arm's throw (Throwing: its pack reads it); the clip's marker,
# frag.release, lets go of the one carried, with 2.5 s to live (without the marker, a moment later).
#
# The grenade going off: when its time is up, every simulation sets it off where it is, on the same
# tick (so the blast is never a lead late). The numbers are the frag mod's (its options, published
# as fields); hurting who is near is the mod's too (combat.damage).

# The throw lets go at the arm clip's frag.release marker, or a moment later without it (a body the
# arm's pack does not fit, the critter): a throw always happens.
motion Throwing on use
	item carried
	cooldown 0.7
	lasts 0.7
	then when frag.release within 0.3
	emit frag.thrown
	launch carried speed 15 lift 3 ahead 0.8 seconds 2.5
	timeout
	emit frag.thrown
	launch carried speed 15 lift 3 ahead 0.8 seconds 2.5

motion Blast on expired
	owner item
	emit frag.blast
	break radius frag.break_radius speed frag.push seconds frag.rubble_seconds
	find Blast bodies
		impulse (distance < frag.radius) * frag.push * (1 - distance / frag.radius) along away
		impulse (distance < frag.radius) * 0.35 * frag.push * (1 - distance / frag.radius) along up
